<!--
  <error-boundary> — L1 language element (element mode). RENDER-FAULT ISOLATION.

  Wraps a subtree. If a render expression, a controller `mount()`, or a reactive
  effect inside it throws, the throw is routed here (the runtime walks up the DOM
  from the fault site via `findNearestBoundary`), the protected content is pulled
  out of view, and the fallback takes its place. Innermost boundary wins, exactly
  like React: the walk stops at the first ancestor carrying a handle.

    <error-boundary message="This panel could not load.">
      <risky-widget></risky-widget>
    </error-boundary>

  Supply your own fallback with the named slot; otherwise the built-in one
  renders (destructive tone, message, Retry):

    <error-boundary>
      <risky-widget></risky-widget>
      <div slot="fallback">…your markup…</div>
    </error-boundary>

  Recovery: the built-in Retry button resets the boundary, as does `el.reset()`.
  A reset clears the caught state and the content returns.

  WHY THIS IS AN ELEMENT AND NOT A COMPONENT. It was a component (a controller
  holding a `caughtError` signal, with `{#if caught}fallback{:else}<slot/>{/if}`).
  The reason to keep it one would be REAL unmount — disposing the broken subtree's
  reactive scope. But the protected content arrives through a `<slot>`, which means
  it belongs to the PARENT's scope, not the boundary's; swapping the branch merely
  stopped projecting it. Nothing was ever disposed. So the controller bought a
  custom element, a signal, and a scope in exchange for nothing an action cannot
  do, and this element does the same job with no component overhead at all.

  What that costs, stated plainly: effects inside the protected subtree are NOT
  disposed — they belong to the enclosing component and keep their scope. If one
  throws again it re-enters `catch`, where the re-entrancy guard makes it a no-op
  (the first error is the one that is reported). True disposal would require
  locating the boundary by OWNER CHAIN rather than by DOM walk — Solid's model —
  which is a runtime change, not an element change.

  The visible message is the `message` prop (default "Something went wrong"), NOT
  the raw error text: an exception string is for a developer, not a user. The real
  error rides the `sui-error-caught` event detail and the `data-error-message`
  attribute.
-->

<component>{
  props: {
    message: '',
  },
}</component>

<style>
  /* A healthy boundary is INVISIBLE to layout: `display: contents` on both the
     root and the content wrapper means the protected children lay out against
     the boundary's parent exactly as if the boundary were not in the tree. That
     matters — a boundary is something you wrap around things you already laid
     out, and it must not disturb them. `display: none` beats `contents` on the
     caught branch, so the swap is a pure CSS gate on [data-caught]. */
  .sui-error-boundary { display: contents; }
  .sui-error-boundary > .content { display: contents; }
  .sui-error-boundary[data-caught] > .content { display: none; }

  .sui-error-boundary > .fallback { display: none; }
  .sui-error-boundary[data-caught] > .fallback {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    background: var(--tone-destructive-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--tone-destructive-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-xs);
    color: var(--foreground);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .sui-error-boundary .fallback-icon {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: var(--icon-size-lg);
    height: var(--icon-size-lg);
    color: var(--destructive);
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
  }

  .sui-error-boundary .fallback-body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-1);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .sui-error-boundary .message {
    color: var(--muted-foreground);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
  }

  .sui-error-boundary .retry {
    align-self: flex-start;
    background: var(--surface-1);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-3);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--foreground);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-xs);
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .sui-error-boundary .retry:hover { background: var(--muted); }
  .sui-error-boundary .retry:focus-visible {
    outline: var(--focus-ring);
    outline-offset: var(--focus-ring-offset);
  }

  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    .sui-error-boundary[data-caught] > .fallback { border-color: CanvasText; }
    .sui-error-boundary .fallback-icon { color: CanvasText; }
  }
</style>

<div use:errorBoundary>
  <div class="content"><slot></slot></div>
  <div class="fallback" role="alert">
    <slot name="fallback">
      <svg class="fallback-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M10.3 3.9 1.8 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.7 3h17a2 2 0 0 0 1.7-3L13.7 3.9a2 2 0 0 0-3.4 0Z"></path>
        <path d="M12 9v4"></path>
        <path d="M12 17h.01"></path>
      </svg>
      <div class="fallback-body">
        <div class="message">Something went wrong</div>
        <button class="retry" type="button">Retry</button>
      </div>
    </slot>
  </div>
</div>
